/* Pharmacology 7: Cardiovascular & renal */
Z("pharm7",0,"ph",["ACE inhibitors (captopril, enalapril, lisinopril, ramipril) block conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, lowering BP and protecting heart and kidneys; they are used in hypertension, heart failure, post-MI and diabetic nephropathy.","Angiotensin II is the body's 'squeeze and hold salt' hormone; ACE inhibitors cut its supply. Bradykinin builds up as a side effect — causing cough.",["MOA: inhibit ACE (kininase II) → ↓ Ang II (vasodilatation, ↓ aldosterone) and ↑ bradykinin (vasodilatation, cough, angioedema).","Uses: hypertension (esp. with diabetes, CKD, HF), CHF (↓ mortality), post-MI (prevents remodelling), diabetic nephropathy (↓ proteinuria), scleroderma renal crisis.","ADR 'CAPTOPRIL': Cough, Angioedema, Proteinuria, Taste change, hypOtension (first dose), Pregnancy (fetotoxic), Rash, Increased K⁺, Leucopenia; also ↑ creatinine.","CI: pregnancy, bilateral renal artery stenosis, history of angioedema, hyperkalaemia; avoid with K⁺-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs."],"ACEI → cough (bradykinin); switch to ARB."],
"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors are a cornerstone of cardiovascular therapy. Drugs: captopril (sulfhydryl, short-acting, active form), enalapril, ramipril, perindopril, fosinopril, trandolapril, benazepril (prodrugs converted in liver), lisinopril (active, not metabolised, renal excretion). Mechanism: inhibit ACE (dipeptidyl carboxypeptidase/kininase II), blocking conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II and degradation of bradykinin. Consequences: reduced angiotensin II → arteriolar and venous dilatation (reduces afterload and preload), reduced aldosterone (natriuresis, K⁺ retention), reduced sympathetic activity, reduced ADH and thirst, reversal of cardiac and vascular remodelling (hypertrophy, fibrosis), efferent arteriolar dilatation in kidney (lowers intraglomerular pressure — renoprotection); increased bradykinin → vasodilatation (NO, prostaglandins), cough, angioedema. No reflex tachycardia; preserve exercise capacity; no adverse metabolic effects (improve insulin sensitivity). Pharmacokinetics: oral; prodrugs (enalapril → enalaprilat); mostly renal excretion (dose adjust); IV enalaprilat available. Uses: (1) hypertension — first-line, especially with diabetes, CKD, heart failure, post-MI, LVH; (2) congestive heart failure — reduce symptoms, hospitalisation and mortality in HFrEF; (3) myocardial infarction — started within 24 h in stable patients, prevent ventricular remodelling, reduce mortality; (4) prophylaxis in high cardiovascular risk (ramipril — HOPE trial); (5) diabetic and non-diabetic nephropathy — reduce proteinuria, slow progression; (6) scleroderma renal crisis; (7) Bartter syndrome. Adverse effects: hypotension (first-dose, especially in volume-depleted, on diuretics, high renin states), dry persistent cough (5–20%; bradykinin/substance P; switch to ARB), hyperkalaemia (especially with renal impairment, K⁺ supplements, K⁺-sparing diuretics), acute kidney injury in bilateral renal artery stenosis or stenosis in a solitary kidney (efferent tone dependence), angioedema (rare but potentially fatal; more in black patients), rashes, urticaria, dysgeusia (captopril), neutropenia and proteinuria (captopril at high doses), fetopathic (second and third trimester — oligohydramnios, renal dysgenesis, skull hypoplasia, growth retardation) — contraindicated in pregnancy, headache, dizziness. Interactions: NSAIDs blunt effect and worsen renal function; K⁺-sparing diuretics and supplements (hyperkalaemia); lithium (raised levels); sacubitril (angioedema — 36-h gap). Contraindications: pregnancy, bilateral renal artery stenosis, angioedema history, hyperkalaemia.",
[["Drugs",["Captopril (active, short-acting, SH group)","Enalapril, ramipril, perindopril (prodrugs)","Lisinopril (active, long-acting)","Enalaprilat (IV)"]],
["Uses",["Hypertension (with diabetes, CKD, HF)","Heart failure (↓ mortality)","Post-MI remodelling","Diabetic nephropathy","Scleroderma renal crisis"]],
["Adverse effects",[["ADR","Note"],["Dry cough","Bradykinin; switch to ARB"],["Angioedema","Stop permanently"],["Hyperkalaemia","Monitor K⁺"],["First-dose hypotension","Start low, stop diuretic"],["Acute renal failure","Bilateral RAS"],["Fetotoxicity","CI in pregnancy"],["Dysgeusia, rash, neutropenia","Captopril"]]]],
{f:"Angiotensinogen → (renin) → Ang I → (ACE) → Ang II → AT1 → vasoconstriction, aldosterone, remodelling\n ACE also degrades bradykinin\n ACEI: ↓ Ang II + ↑ bradykinin → ↓ BP, ↓ remodelling, cough",c:"ACE inhibitors lower BP and provide cardio-renal protection; monitoring of K⁺ and creatinine and avoidance in pregnancy are essential."});

Z("pharm7",1,"ph",["Angiotensin receptor blockers (losartan, telmisartan, valsartan) block angiotensin II at AT1 receptors; they act like ACE inhibitors but do not raise bradykinin, so they do not cause cough.","Instead of cutting supply of angiotensin II (ACEI), ARBs block its landing site (AT1 receptor).",["Drugs: losartan, valsartan, telmisartan, olmesartan, irbesartan, candesartan, azilsartan.","MOA: selective AT1 blockade → vasodilatation, ↓ aldosterone; AT2 stimulation unopposed; no bradykinin rise.","Uses: hypertension, HF (ACEI-intolerant; sacubitril/valsartan), diabetic nephropathy, post-MI, stroke prevention (LVH — losartan); losartan is uricosuric.","ADR: hypotension, hyperkalaemia, renal impairment, fetotoxicity (CI pregnancy), dizziness; cough and angioedema much rarer than ACEI."],"ARB = 'ACEI without the cough'."],
"Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs, 'sartans') — losartan, valsartan, candesartan, irbesartan, telmisartan, olmesartan, eprosartan, azilsartan — selectively and competitively (some insurmountably) block the AT1 receptor, which mediates nearly all known actions of angiotensin II: vasoconstriction, aldosterone and ADH release, sympathetic facilitation, renal Na⁺ reabsorption, cell growth and fibrosis. Differences from ACE inhibitors: block effects of angiotensin II produced by non-ACE pathways (chymase); do not inhibit bradykinin degradation, so cough and angioedema are much less frequent; leave AT2 receptors unblocked (raised Ang II stimulates AT2 — possibly vasodilatory, antiproliferative). Pharmacokinetics: oral; losartan is converted to a more potent active metabolite (EXP-3174); telmisartan has the longest t½ (24 h) and partial PPAR-γ agonism; candesartan cilexetil and olmesartan medoxomil are prodrugs. Uses: hypertension (first-line, similar efficacy to ACEI; preferred when ACEI cough occurs), heart failure with reduced EF (ACEI-intolerant; valsartan with sacubitril — ARNI — superior to enalapril), post-MI LV dysfunction (valsartan), diabetic nephropathy (losartan, irbesartan — reduce progression), LVH with hypertension (losartan reduces stroke — LIFE trial), Marfan syndrome (losartan, TGF-β), hyperuricaemia with hypertension (losartan is uricosuric). Adverse effects: hypotension (volume depletion), hyperkalaemia, renal dysfunction in bilateral renal artery stenosis, dizziness, headache, fatigue, fetotoxicity (contraindicated in pregnancy), rarely angioedema, sprue-like enteropathy (olmesartan). Combination of ACEI + ARB is not recommended (more hyperkalaemia and renal failure without added benefit).",
[["ACEI vs ARB",[["Feature","ACE inhibitor","ARB"],["Site","Blocks Ang II formation","Blocks AT1 receptor"],["Bradykinin","↑","No change"],["Cough","5–20%","Rare"],["Angioedema","More","Rare"],["Non-ACE Ang II","Not blocked","Blocked"],["Pregnancy","Contraindicated","Contraindicated"],["Hyperkalaemia","Yes","Yes"]]],
["Uses",["Hypertension","Heart failure (ARNI: sacubitril/valsartan)","Diabetic nephropathy","LVH, stroke prevention (losartan)","Marfan syndrome (losartan)"]]],
{c:"ARBs provide the benefits of RAS blockade without bradykinin-mediated cough; they share hyperkalaemia, renal and fetal precautions with ACE inhibitors."});

Z("pharm7",2,"ph",["Heart failure drugs reduce symptoms and prolong life: diuretics relieve congestion; ACEI/ARB/ARNI, β-blockers, MRAs and SGLT2 inhibitors reduce mortality; digoxin increases contractility and controls heart rate in AF; vasodilators like hydralazine + nitrate reduce load on the heart.","Digoxin is like a stronger push from a tired pump; diuretics drain excess water; vasodilators open the road so the pump works less.",["Digitalis (digoxin) MOA: inhibits Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase → ↑ intracellular Na⁺ → ↓ Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchange → ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ → ↑ contractility (positive inotropy); vagal stimulation → ↓ HR, ↓ AV conduction.","Uses: HFrEF with AF (rate control), symptomatic HF despite therapy; AF/atrial flutter rate control. Narrow TI (0.5–0.9 ng/mL).","Toxicity: anorexia, nausea, vomiting, xanthopsia (yellow vision), arrhythmias (ventricular bigeminy, PAT with block, AV block), hyperkalaemia (acute); precipitated by hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypercalcaemia, renal failure, quinidine, verapamil, amiodarone. Treatment: stop drug, K⁺, lignocaine/phenytoin, atropine, digoxin-specific Fab antibodies.","Vasodilators: hydralazine (arteriolar — ↓ afterload) + isosorbide dinitrate (venous — ↓ preload) → ↓ mortality in black patients/ACEI-intolerant; nitroprusside, nitroglycerin in acute HF."],"Digoxin toxicity ↑ with 'Low K⁺, low Mg²⁺, high Ca²⁺'."],
"Congestive heart failure (CHF) is a state in which the heart cannot pump blood adequately to meet tissue needs; most commonly heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF). Drugs for CHF: (1) drugs that reduce mortality and progression (neurohormonal blockade) — RAS inhibitors: ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan — preferred); β-blockers (carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol, nebivolol — started low and titrated slowly in stable patients); mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists (spironolactone, eplerenone); SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin) — these four classes form 'quadruple therapy'; ivabradine (If channel blocker, HR > 70 in sinus rhythm); hydralazine–isosorbide dinitrate; vericiguat; (2) drugs for symptomatic relief — diuretics (furosemide, torsemide; thiazides add-on), digoxin; (3) inotropes for acute decompensated HF — dobutamine, dopamine, milrinone (PDE3 inhibitor), levosimendan (Ca²⁺ sensitiser); (4) vasodilators — nitroglycerin, nitroprusside, nesiritide in acute HF; hydralazine + nitrates chronically. Cardiac glycosides (digitalis): digoxin (from Digitalis lanata), digitoxin, ouabain. Pharmacological actions: (a) heart — positive inotropic effect: digoxin binds and inhibits the α subunit of membrane Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase; intracellular Na⁺ rises, reducing Ca²⁺ extrusion by the Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger; more Ca²⁺ is stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum and released with each beat — increased force of contraction, increased cardiac output, reduced end-diastolic pressure and heart size, improved diuresis in failing heart; negative chronotropic — reduced heart rate by vagal stimulation, baroreceptor sensitisation and reduced sympathetic tone (improved haemodynamics); electrophysiological — prolongs AV nodal refractory period and slows AV conduction (vagal) — rate control in AF; shortens atrial and ventricular refractory period; increases automaticity at toxic doses (delayed afterdepolarisations) — ectopics; ECG — prolonged PR, ST depression ('reverse tick/sagging'), T-wave flattening or inversion, short QT; (b) kidney — diuresis secondary to improved circulation; (c) CNS — CTZ stimulation (nausea, vomiting), visual disturbances, confusion. Pharmacokinetics of digoxin: oral bioavailability 60–80%, large Vd (skeletal muscle binding), 25% protein bound, excreted unchanged by kidney (dose reduction in renal failure, elderly), t½ 36–40 h (steady state in about a week; loading dose possible), narrow therapeutic range 0.5–0.9 ng/mL (toxicity > 2 ng/mL), P-gp substrate. Uses: (1) HFrEF — symptomatic patients despite optimal therapy (reduces hospitalisation, not mortality), especially with atrial fibrillation; (2) atrial fibrillation and flutter — ventricular rate control (with β-blocker; slow onset, ineffective during exercise); (3) PSVT (rarely now). Adverse effects and toxicity: extracardiac — anorexia, nausea, vomiting (early signs), diarrhoea, abdominal pain, fatigue, headache, confusion, delirium (elderly), visual disturbances (blurred, yellow/green vision — xanthopsia, halos), gynaecomastia; cardiac — almost any arrhythmia: ventricular extrasystoles (bigeminy), ventricular tachycardia/fibrillation, paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with AV block (characteristic), AV block of all degrees, sinus bradycardia; acute overdose — hyperkalaemia (pump block). Predisposing factors: hypokalaemia (diuretics; K⁺ competes with digoxin for the pump), hypomagnesaemia, hypercalcaemia, hypothyroidism, renal impairment, old age, myocardial ischaemia, hypoxia; drug interactions — quinidine, verapamil, amiodarone, macrolides, propafenone (raise digoxin levels via P-gp/renal clearance), diuretics (hypokalaemia), β-blockers and CCBs (additive AV block), sympathomimetics, succinylcholine (arrhythmias), antacids and cholestyramine (reduce absorption). Treatment of toxicity: stop digoxin; check K⁺, Mg²⁺, renal function, digoxin level; oral/IV potassium if hypokalaemic (not in AV block or hyperkalaemia); ventricular arrhythmias — lignocaine or phenytoin (do not worsen AV block); bradycardia and AV block — atropine, temporary pacing; life-threatening toxicity, K⁺ > 5 mEq/L, or massive overdose — digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Digibind/DigiFab); avoid DC cardioversion (ventricular fibrillation) and calcium. Contraindications: hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, WPW syndrome with AF, partial AV block, ventricular tachycardia, myocarditis, HFpEF. Vasodilators in CHF: reduce preload (venodilators — nitrates; relieve pulmonary congestion), afterload (arteriolar dilators — hydralazine; increase stroke volume) or both (ACEI, ARB, nitroprusside, prazosin). Hydralazine: direct arteriolar smooth muscle relaxant (NO release, inhibition of IP3-mediated Ca²⁺ release); reflex tachycardia, fluid retention, lupus-like syndrome in slow acetylators, headache; in CHF, combination hydralazine + isosorbide dinitrate (A-HeFT) reduces mortality in African-American patients and is used in patients intolerant of ACEI/ARB (renal failure, hyperkalaemia); nitrates also prevent hydralazine tolerance. Nitroprusside is used in acute HF with hypertension (balanced dilator).",
[["Drugs for CHF",[["Group","Drugs","Effect"],["RAS blockers","ACEI, ARB, ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan)","↓ Mortality"],["β-blockers","Carvedilol, metoprolol, bisoprolol","↓ Mortality"],["MRAs","Spironolactone, eplerenone","↓ Mortality"],["SGLT2 inhibitors","Dapagliflozin, empagliflozin","↓ Mortality, hospitalisation"],["Diuretics","Furosemide, torsemide","Symptom relief"],["Digoxin","—","↓ Hospitalisation; rate control"],["Vasodilators","Hydralazine + ISDN, nitrates","↓ Mortality (selected)"],["Inotropes","Dobutamine, milrinone, levosimendan","Acute HF"],["Others","Ivabradine, vericiguat","Selected patients"]]],
["Digoxin pharmacology",[["Feature","Detail"],["MOA","Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase inhibition → ↑ Ca²⁺ → ↑ contractility; vagomimetic"],["Kinetics","Renal excretion, t½ 40 h, TI narrow 0.5–0.9 ng/mL"],["Uses","HFrEF with AF, AF rate control"],["ECG","Sagging ST, short QT, ↑ PR"],["Toxicity","GI, xanthopsia, bigeminy, PAT with block, AV block"],["Antidote","Digoxin-specific Fab"]]],
["Factors increasing digoxin toxicity",["Hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypercalcaemia","Renal failure, elderly","Hypothyroidism, ischaemia","Quinidine, verapamil, amiodarone, macrolides"]]],
{i:"Modern management of HFrEF rests on four disease-modifying drug classes; digoxin and diuretics provide symptomatic relief.",x:[["Treatment of digoxin toxicity",["Stop drug, monitor ECG","Correct K⁺/Mg²⁺","Lignocaine/phenytoin for ventricular arrhythmias","Atropine/pacing for bradycardia","Digoxin Fab for severe toxicity"]]],f:"Digoxin → inhibits Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase → ↑ [Na⁺]i → ↓ Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchange → ↑ [Ca²⁺]i → ↑ contractility\n + vagal ↑ → ↓ HR, ↓ AV conduction (AF rate control)\n Toxicity: DADs → arrhythmias (worse with ↓ K⁺)",c:"Digoxin improves symptoms and controls ventricular rate but not mortality; its narrow therapeutic index demands attention to electrolytes, renal function and interactions, while neurohormonal blockers form the core of HF therapy."});

Z("pharm7",3,"ph",["Antiarrhythmic drugs restore normal heart rhythm by acting on ion channels or receptors; the Vaughan Williams classification groups them as Na⁺ channel blockers (I), β-blockers (II), K⁺ channel blockers (III) and Ca²⁺ channel blockers (IV).","Each class blocks a different 'gate' in the cardiac action potential: sodium (fast upstroke), beta (sympathetic drive), potassium (repolarisation length), calcium (AV node).",["Class I: IA (quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide), IB (lignocaine, mexiletine, phenytoin), IC (flecainide, propafenone); II: propranolol, esmolol, metoprolol; III: amiodarone, dronedarone, sotalol, ibutilide, dofetilide; IV: verapamil, diltiazem; others: adenosine, digoxin, atropine, MgSO4.","Lignocaine (IB): blocks inactivated Na⁺ channels in ischaemic tissue; IV only (high first pass); uses — ventricular arrhythmias post-MI, digoxin toxicity; ADR — CNS (drowsiness, paraesthesia, seizures).","Amiodarone (III, all classes): prolongs APD/ERP; broad spectrum — AF, VT/VF; ADR — pulmonary fibrosis, hypo/hyperthyroidism, corneal microdeposits, photosensitivity, blue-grey skin, hepatotoxicity, QT prolongation; long t½ (weeks).","Verapamil (IV): blocks L-type Ca²⁺ in SA/AV node; uses — PSVT termination, AF rate control; ADR — bradycardia, AV block, constipation, hypotension; CI — WPW with AF, with β-blockers (IV), HF."],"Classes 'Some Block Potassium Channels': Sodium, Beta, Potassium, Calcium."],
"Antiarrhythmic drugs suppress abnormal impulse generation (automaticity, triggered activity) or abnormal conduction (re-entry). Vaughan Williams classification (modified): Class I — Na⁺ channel blockers (membrane stabilisers): IA (moderate Na⁺ block, prolong APD and ERP by K⁺ block) — quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide; IB (weak Na⁺ block with fast kinetics, shorten APD, act on depolarised/ischaemic tissue) — lignocaine, mexiletine, tocainide, phenytoin; IC (marked Na⁺ block, slow dissociation, little effect on APD, marked QRS widening) — flecainide, propafenone, encainide; Class II — β-adrenergic blockers: propranolol, esmolol, metoprolol, atenolol, sotalol (also III); Class III — K⁺ channel blockers (prolong APD and ERP): amiodarone, dronedarone, sotalol, ibutilide, dofetilide, bretylium, vernakalant; Class IV — Ca²⁺ channel blockers: verapamil, diltiazem; Others (class V/unclassified): adenosine (A1 agonist — opens K⁺ channels in AV node), digoxin, atropine, magnesium sulphate (torsades), ivabradine (If blocker). Lignocaine: mechanism — blocks open and inactivated Na⁺ channels with rapid recovery; preferentially affects ischaemic, partially depolarised Purkinje and ventricular tissue (use-dependent), raises threshold and suppresses automaticity, little effect on atria and normal tissue, minimal effect on ECG; pharmacokinetics — extensive first-pass metabolism (not oral), given IV (bolus 1–1.5 mg/kg then infusion 1–4 mg/min), short t½ (90 min; prolonged in HF and liver disease); uses — ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation (especially after MI, cardiac surgery; amiodarone now preferred in cardiac arrest), digitalis-induced ventricular arrhythmias, local anaesthesia; adverse effects — CNS (drowsiness, dizziness, paraesthesia, tinnitus, slurred speech, muscle twitching, convulsions), hypotension, bradycardia, AV block at high doses; least cardiodepressant among antiarrhythmics. Amiodarone: iodine-containing benzofuran; mechanism — primarily blocks K⁺ channels (IKr) prolonging APD and ERP in all cardiac tissues (class III), also blocks Na⁺ channels (I), Ca²⁺ channels (IV) and is non-competitive α/β blocker (II); slows SA rate and AV conduction; coronary and peripheral vasodilator; despite QT prolongation, torsades is rare; pharmacokinetics — variable oral bioavailability (30–50%), very lipophilic, huge Vd (accumulates in fat, liver, lung, skin), very long t½ (25–100 days), loading dose needed, metabolised by CYP3A4 to active desethylamiodarone; inhibits CYP2C9, 2D6, 3A4 and P-gp (raises warfarin, digoxin, statins levels); uses — broad-spectrum: ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation (including resuscitation — IV 300 mg bolus), recurrent VT prevention, atrial fibrillation (cardioversion and maintenance of sinus rhythm, rate control in HF), atrial flutter, WPW-related arrhythmias, arrhythmias in heart failure (no negative inotropy); adverse effects (dose and duration related) — cardiac: bradycardia, AV block, QT prolongation, hypotension (IV), heart failure; pulmonary: interstitial pneumonitis and pulmonary fibrosis (most serious; can be fatal); thyroid: hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism (iodine content, inhibits T4 → T3 conversion); eye: corneal microdeposits (nearly universal, reversible), optic neuropathy; skin: photosensitivity, blue-grey (slate) discolouration; liver: raised transaminases, hepatitis, cirrhosis; neurological: tremor, ataxia, peripheral neuropathy; GI: nausea, constipation; epididymitis; monitoring — TFTs, LFTs, chest X-ray, PFT, eye exam baseline and periodically. Dronedarone is a non-iodinated analogue with fewer thyroid/pulmonary effects but less efficacy (contraindicated in severe HF). Verapamil: phenylalkylamine CCB; mechanism — blocks L-type voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels (use-dependent) in SA and AV nodes, reducing SA rate, slowing AV conduction and prolonging AV nodal ERP; negative inotropic; vasodilator; uses — termination of PSVT (AV nodal re-entry; IV 5 mg over 2–3 min; adenosine preferred), ventricular rate control in AF and atrial flutter, prophylaxis of PSVT, angina (variant, chronic stable), hypertension, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, migraine/cluster headache prophylaxis; adverse effects — constipation (most common), bradycardia, AV block, hypotension, heart failure, flushing, headache, ankle oedema, gingival hyperplasia, raises digoxin levels; contraindications — sick sinus syndrome, AV block, HFrEF, WPW with AF (accelerates accessory pathway conduction → VF), VT (wide-complex tachycardia may collapse), IV with β-blockers (asystole). Proarrhythmia is a common risk of antiarrhythmics (CAST trial — IC drugs increased mortality post-MI), so non-pharmacological options (ablation, ICD) are increasingly used.",
[["Vaughan Williams classification",[["Class","Mechanism","Drugs","ECG"],["IA","Na⁺ block (moderate) + K⁺ block","Quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide","↑ QRS, ↑ QT"],["IB","Na⁺ block (weak), shorten APD","Lignocaine, mexiletine, phenytoin","Minimal"],["IC","Na⁺ block (marked)","Flecainide, propafenone","↑↑ QRS"],["II","β-blockade","Propranolol, esmolol, metoprolol","↑ PR"],["III","K⁺ block, ↑ APD","Amiodarone, sotalol, ibutilide, dofetilide","↑ QT"],["IV","Ca²⁺ block","Verapamil, diltiazem","↑ PR"],["Others","—","Adenosine, digoxin, MgSO4, atropine","—"]]],
["Comparison of key drugs",[["Feature","Lignocaine","Amiodarone","Verapamil"],["Class","IB","III (+I, II, IV)","IV"],["Route","IV only","Oral, IV","Oral, IV"],["Main use","Ventricular arrhythmias","AF, VT/VF","PSVT, AF rate"],["t½","90 min","Weeks","4–6 h"],["Key ADR","CNS toxicity, seizures","Pulmonary fibrosis, thyroid, corneal deposits","Constipation, AV block, HF"]]],
["Drug of choice",[["Arrhythmia","Drug"],["PSVT (acute)","Adenosine (verapamil alternative)"],["AF rate control","β-blocker, diltiazem/verapamil, digoxin"],["AF rhythm control","Amiodarone, flecainide, dronedarone"],["VT/VF","Amiodarone, lignocaine; DC shock"],["Torsades de pointes","Magnesium sulphate"],["Digoxin-induced","Lignocaine, phenytoin, Fab"],["Sinus bradycardia","Atropine"]]]],
{i:"Antiarrhythmic drugs act on the phases of the cardiac action potential; all can be proarrhythmic, so careful selection is required.",f:"Cardiac action potential:\n Phase 0 (Na⁺ in) ← Class I\n Phase 2 (Ca²⁺ in) ← Class IV (nodal phase 0)\n Phase 3 (K⁺ out) ← Class III\n Phase 4 (automaticity) ← Class II (↓ sympathetic)\n Adenosine → ↑ K⁺ in AV node",c:"Lignocaine, amiodarone and verapamil illustrate the different classes: lignocaine for ventricular arrhythmias, amiodarone as a broad-spectrum agent with organ toxicity, and verapamil for supraventricular arrhythmias."});

Z("pharm7",4,"ph",["Antianginal drugs relieve chest pain from myocardial ischaemia by reducing the heart's oxygen demand and/or increasing supply: nitrates, β-blockers and calcium channel blockers are main groups.","Angina is a mismatch — heart demands more oxygen than narrowed arteries can deliver. Nitrates reduce workload (venodilatation) and relieve spasm; β-blockers slow the heart; CCBs relax arteries.",["Classification: nitrates (GTN, ISDN, ISMN), β-blockers (atenolol, metoprolol), CCBs (amlodipine, nifedipine, verapamil, diltiazem), K⁺ channel opener (nicorandil), others (ranolazine, ivabradine, trimetazidine), antiplatelets/statins (prevent MI).","Nitrates MOA: release NO → ↑ guanylyl cyclase → ↑ cGMP → myosin light chain dephosphorylation → venodilatation (↓ preload) > arteriolar dilatation; relieve coronary spasm.","Nitrate ADR: headache, flushing, postural hypotension, reflex tachycardia, tolerance (nitrate-free interval 8–12 h), methaemoglobinaemia; CI with sildenafil.","CCBs: block L-type Ca²⁺ channels → vasodilatation (↓ afterload), coronary dilatation; verapamil/diltiazem also ↓ HR and contractility; ADR — ankle oedema, flushing, headache (DHPs); constipation, bradycardia (verapamil); uses — angina (variant: drug of choice), hypertension, arrhythmias."],"Nitrates need a 'nitrate-free' night to avoid tolerance."],
"Angina pectoris is chest pain due to myocardial ischaemia from imbalance between oxygen demand and supply. Types: classical stable (exertional), variant/Prinzmetal (vasospastic), unstable (acute coronary syndrome). Classification of antianginal drugs: (1) nitrates — short-acting: glyceryl trinitrate (nitroglycerin; sublingual, spray, IV), isosorbide dinitrate (sublingual); long-acting: isosorbide dinitrate (oral), isosorbide-5-mononitrate, erythrityl tetranitrate, pentaerythritol tetranitrate, GTN transdermal patch/ointment; (2) β-adrenergic blockers — propranolol, metoprolol, atenolol, bisoprolol; (3) calcium channel blockers — phenylalkylamine: verapamil; benzothiazepine: diltiazem; dihydropyridines: nifedipine, amlodipine, felodipine, nicardipine, nimodipine, lercanidipine, cilnidipine; (4) potassium channel opener — nicorandil; (5) others — ranolazine (late Na⁺ current inhibitor), ivabradine (If channel inhibitor), trimetazidine (metabolic modulator — pFOX inhibitor), dipyridamole, oxyphedrine; (6) drugs for prevention of MI and death — antiplatelets (aspirin, clopidogrel), statins, ACE inhibitors. Organic nitrates: mechanism — denitrated in smooth muscle (mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase) to release nitric oxide, which activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, increasing cGMP; cGMP activates protein kinase G, causing dephosphorylation of myosin light chains (via MLC phosphatase) and reduced intracellular Ca²⁺ — smooth muscle relaxation; predominantly venodilators at low doses (pooling of blood in veins reduces venous return, preload, ventricular wall tension and oxygen demand), arteriolar dilatation at higher doses (reduces afterload), dilatation of large epicardial coronary arteries and collaterals and relief of spasm (redistribution to ischaemic subendocardium); also relax bronchial, biliary, GI and urinary smooth muscle; inhibit platelet aggregation. Pharmacokinetics: GTN has high first-pass metabolism (oral bioavailability < 10%), so given sublingually (onset 1–2 min, duration 20–30 min); ISMN has no first pass (oral, once or twice daily); transdermal and IV forms. Uses: acute attack of angina (sublingual GTN or ISDN) and prophylaxis before exertion; chronic stable angina prophylaxis (long-acting nitrates with nitrate-free interval); variant angina; unstable angina and NSTEMI (IV GTN); acute LVF/pulmonary oedema; hypertensive emergencies (perioperative); oesophageal spasm and biliary colic; cyanide poisoning (amyl nitrite — methaemoglobin formation); anal fissure (GTN ointment). Adverse effects: throbbing headache (most common; meningeal vasodilatation), flushing, dizziness, postural hypotension, syncope, reflex tachycardia and palpitation, rebound angina on sudden withdrawal, methaemoglobinaemia (high doses), rashes; tolerance — develops with continuous exposure (depletion of sulfhydryl groups, ROS, neurohormonal counter-regulation); prevented by 8–12 hour nitrate-free interval daily (e.g., eccentric dosing of ISMN, removing patch at night); interaction — PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil) cause severe hypotension — contraindicated within 24–48 h; contraindicated in hypotension, raised ICP, HOCM, severe aortic stenosis, right ventricular infarction. Calcium channel blockers: mechanism — block L-type voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels in vascular smooth muscle and heart, reducing Ca²⁺ entry; DHPs are selective for vascular smooth muscle (arteriolar dilatation, reduced afterload, coronary dilatation; reflex tachycardia with short-acting nifedipine), verapamil is more cardioselective (reduced HR, contractility, AV conduction), diltiazem intermediate; in angina they reduce oxygen demand (afterload, HR and contractility) and increase supply (coronary dilatation, relief of spasm). Uses: angina — variant (drug of choice), chronic stable (amlodipine, diltiazem; with β-blocker — DHP only), hypertension (amlodipine first-line), arrhythmias (verapamil, diltiazem — PSVT, AF), hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (verapamil), subarachnoid haemorrhage (nimodipine — prevents vasospasm), Raynaud's phenomenon, preterm labour (nifedipine), migraine prophylaxis (flunarizine, verapamil), oesophageal spasm. Adverse effects: DHPs — headache, flushing, dizziness, palpitations and reflex tachycardia (short-acting nifedipine may increase mortality in MI — avoid), ankle oedema (precapillary dilatation; not diuretic responsive), gum hyperplasia, hypotension; verapamil/diltiazem — bradycardia, AV block, heart failure, constipation (verapamil), hypotension; drug interactions — verapamil raises digoxin levels, IV verapamil with β-blockers dangerous; CYP3A4 substrates (grapefruit juice). β-blockers in angina: reduce HR, contractility and BP → reduce oxygen demand, prolong diastole (better coronary perfusion); first-line for chronic stable angina and post-MI; not for variant angina (unopposed α vasoconstriction).",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Nitrates","GTN, ISDN, ISMN"],["β-blockers","Metoprolol, atenolol, bisoprolol"],["CCBs","Amlodipine, nifedipine, verapamil, diltiazem"],["K⁺ channel opener","Nicorandil"],["Others","Ranolazine, ivabradine, trimetazidine"]]],
["Nitrates vs CCBs",[["Feature","Nitrates","CCBs"],["MOA","NO → cGMP","Block L-type Ca²⁺ channel"],["Main effect","Venodilatation (↓ preload)","Arteriolar dilatation (↓ afterload); ↓ HR (non-DHP)"],["Acute attack","Yes (SL GTN)","No"],["Variant angina","Effective","Drug of choice"],["Tolerance","Yes","No"],["Key ADR","Headache, hypotension, tachycardia","Ankle oedema, flushing; constipation, bradycardia (verapamil)"],["Interaction","PDE5 inhibitors","Verapamil + β-blocker; digoxin"]]],
["Choice by angina type",[["Type","Preferred"],["Acute attack","Sublingual GTN"],["Stable angina","β-blocker ± CCB/nitrate; aspirin, statin"],["Variant angina","CCB + nitrate (avoid β-blocker)"],["Unstable angina","IV GTN, β-blocker, antiplatelets, heparin"]]]],
{i:"Antianginal therapy aims at relieving symptoms by correcting the oxygen supply–demand imbalance, while antiplatelets and statins prevent infarction and death.",f:"O2 demand ← HR, contractility, wall tension (preload, afterload)\n Nitrates: NO → cGMP → venodilatation → ↓ preload\n β-blockers: ↓ HR, contractility\n CCBs: ↓ afterload, coronary dilatation, ↓ HR (non-DHP)\n O2 supply ↑ ← coronary dilatation, relief of spasm",c:"Nitrates, β-blockers and calcium channel blockers relieve angina by complementary mechanisms; choice depends on angina type and comorbidities, with nitrate-free intervals preventing tolerance."});

Z("pharm7",5,"ph",["Nicorandil is an antianginal drug that opens ATP-sensitive K⁺ channels and also donates nitric oxide, dilating both arteries and veins.","It is a 'two-in-one' drug — part potassium channel opener (arteriolar dilator), part nitrate (venodilator).",["MOA: opens KATP channels → hyperpolarisation → arteriolar dilatation; nitrate moiety → NO → cGMP → venodilatation; also ischaemic preconditioning (cardioprotection).","Uses: chronic stable angina (second-line/add-on), refractory and microvascular angina.","ADR: headache (common at start), flushing, dizziness, hypotension, palpitations, oral/anal/GI ulcers (characteristic), nausea.","No tolerance like nitrates; CI with PDE5 inhibitors, cardiogenic shock, hypotension."],"Nicorandil = 'Nitrate + KATP opener', watch for ulcers."],
"Nicorandil is a nicotinamide nitrate ester with a dual mechanism of action. (1) It opens ATP-sensitive potassium (KATP) channels in vascular smooth muscle, causing K⁺ efflux and hyperpolarisation, which closes voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels and relaxes arterioles (reduces afterload) and coronary resistance vessels; (2) its nitrate moiety releases nitric oxide, activating guanylyl cyclase and increasing cGMP, producing venodilatation (reduces preload) and dilatation of epicardial coronaries. Opening of mitochondrial KATP channels in myocytes mimics ischaemic preconditioning, providing cardioprotection. It produces balanced coronary and peripheral vasodilatation with little effect on contractility or AV conduction; tolerance is less than with nitrates. Pharmacokinetics: well absorbed orally, short t½ (~1 h) but longer pharmacological effect; given 5–20 mg twice daily; IV forms used in some countries for ACS/PCI. Uses: prophylaxis of chronic stable angina in patients not controlled by or intolerant to first-line drugs (β-blockers, CCBs), vasospastic and microvascular angina; adjunct during percutaneous coronary intervention (reduces no-reflow). Adverse effects: headache (most common; start low), flushing, dizziness, weakness, nausea, vomiting, palpitations, hypotension; mucosal ulceration — oral (aphthous), anal, perianal, gastrointestinal, vulvovaginal and skin ulcers, fistulae and perforations (stop drug); corneal ulceration; hyperkalaemia rarely. Contraindications: cardiogenic shock, hypotension, LV failure with low filling pressure, hypovolaemia, concurrent PDE5 inhibitors or riociguat.",
[["Key points",[["Feature","Detail"],["MOA","KATP channel opening + NO donation"],["Effect","Arteriolar + venous dilatation; preconditioning"],["Use","Stable angina (add-on), vasospastic/microvascular angina"],["ADR","Headache, flushing, hypotension, mucosal ulcers"],["CI","PDE5 inhibitors, hypotension, shock"]]]],
{c:"Nicorandil combines KATP channel opening with nitrate action, offering balanced vasodilatation and cardioprotection; mucosal ulceration is a distinctive adverse effect."});

Z("pharm7",6,"ph",["Drug therapy in myocardial infarction aims to relieve pain, restore coronary blood flow quickly (thrombolysis or PCI), prevent clot extension and complications, and reduce future events with long-term medications.","Time is muscle: open the blocked artery fast, thin the blood, rest the heart and then protect it for life.",["Immediate (MONA-BASH style): oxygen if SpO2 < 90%, aspirin 325 mg chewed + P2Y12 inhibitor (clopidogrel/ticagrelor), sublingual/IV nitrates, morphine for pain, anticoagulant (heparin/enoxaparin).","Reperfusion in STEMI: primary PCI (< 90–120 min) preferred; thrombolysis (streptokinase, alteplase, tenecteplase, reteplase) within 12 h (best < 3 h) if PCI unavailable.","Early additions: β-blocker (if no HF/shock), high-intensity statin (atorvastatin 80 mg), ACEI/ARB (within 24 h, esp. LV dysfunction), MRA (EF ≤ 40% with HF/DM).","Long-term secondary prevention: dual antiplatelet therapy 12 months then aspirin lifelong, β-blocker, statin, ACEI, lifestyle; treat complications — arrhythmias (amiodarone, lignocaine), HF (diuretics), shock (inotropes)."],"MI therapy: 'Open artery, Antiplatelet, Anticoagulant, β-blocker, ACEI, Statin'."],
"Myocardial infarction (MI) results from occlusive thrombus formation over a ruptured atherosclerotic plaque; treatment aims to limit infarct size, prevent complications and death, and prevent recurrence. (A) Immediate general measures: hospitalise in CCU with ECG monitoring, IV access, rest; oxygen only if hypoxaemic (SpO2 < 90%) or in respiratory distress; analgesia — IV morphine 2–4 mg repeated (relieves pain and anxiety, reduces sympathetic drive and preload) with antiemetic; sublingual GTN 0.4 mg every 5 minutes for 3 doses, then IV GTN for ongoing ischaemia, hypertension or pulmonary oedema (avoid in hypotension, RV infarct, recent PDE5 inhibitor); anxiolytic (diazepam) if needed. (B) Antiplatelet therapy: aspirin 162–325 mg chewed immediately, then 75–150 mg daily lifelong (reduces mortality by ~23%); P2Y12 inhibitor loading — clopidogrel 300–600 mg, ticagrelor 180 mg or prasugrel 60 mg — then maintenance (dual antiplatelet therapy for 12 months); GP IIb/IIIa antagonists (abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban) in high-risk PCI. (C) Anticoagulants: unfractionated heparin, enoxaparin or fondaparinux; bivalirudin during PCI. (D) Reperfusion therapy (STEMI): primary percutaneous coronary intervention (angioplasty with stenting) within 90 minutes of first medical contact (120 minutes if transfer) is preferred; fibrinolytic therapy if PCI not available in time, ideally within 30 minutes of arrival and within 12 hours of symptom onset (maximum benefit within 3 hours) — streptokinase 1.5 million U IV over 60 minutes (antigenic, cheaper; used in India), alteplase (tPA; accelerated regimen), reteplase, tenecteplase (single bolus; fibrin-specific); contraindications — prior haemorrhagic stroke, ischaemic stroke within 3 months, active bleeding, intracranial neoplasm, suspected aortic dissection, recent head trauma or major surgery, uncontrolled hypertension; followed by pharmaco-invasive PCI. NSTEMI/unstable angina: antiplatelets, anticoagulants, early invasive strategy in high-risk patients; fibrinolytics are not indicated. (E) Adjunctive drugs: β-blockers (metoprolol orally within 24 h unless HF, shock, bradycardia, heart block, asthma) — reduce infarct size, arrhythmias, reinfarction and mortality; high-intensity statin (atorvastatin 40–80 mg, rosuvastatin 20–40 mg) early regardless of LDL — plaque stabilisation; ACE inhibitors/ARB within 24 h (especially anterior MI, EF ≤ 40%, HF, diabetes, hypertension) — prevent remodelling, reduce mortality; mineralocorticoid antagonist (eplerenone) in EF ≤ 40% with HF or diabetes; SGLT2 inhibitors in HF; stool softeners. Avoid NSAIDs and short-acting nifedipine. (F) Treatment of complications: ventricular arrhythmias — amiodarone, lignocaine, DC cardioversion/defibrillation; bradycardia/heart block — atropine, temporary pacing; atrial fibrillation — β-blocker, amiodarone, anticoagulation; acute LVF — oxygen, IV furosemide, nitrates, morphine, ventilation; cardiogenic shock — inotropes and vasopressors (noradrenaline, dobutamine), intra-aortic balloon pump, urgent revascularisation; RV infarction — fluids, avoid nitrates; pericarditis — aspirin; LV thrombus — warfarin/DOAC. (G) Secondary prevention (long term): aspirin lifelong (+ P2Y12 inhibitor for 12 months), β-blocker, statin (LDL < 55 mg/dL), ACE inhibitor, glycaemic and BP control, smoking cessation, cardiac rehabilitation, diet and exercise, influenza vaccination.",
[["Immediate management",[["Drug","Dose/notes"],["Aspirin","162–325 mg chewed"],["P2Y12 inhibitor","Clopidogrel 300–600 mg / ticagrelor 180 mg"],["Nitrates","SL GTN 0.4 mg ×3; IV for ongoing pain"],["Morphine","2–4 mg IV for pain"],["Oxygen","If SpO2 < 90%"],["Anticoagulant","UFH / enoxaparin / fondaparinux"]]],
["Reperfusion (STEMI)",[["Option","Notes"],["Primary PCI","Preferred; door-to-balloon < 90 min"],["Fibrinolysis","Within 12 h (best < 3 h); streptokinase, alteplase, tenecteplase"],["Pharmaco-invasive","Lysis then PCI within 3–24 h"]]],
["Long-term secondary prevention",["Aspirin lifelong + P2Y12 inhibitor 12 months","β-blocker","High-intensity statin","ACE inhibitor/ARB","MRA if EF ≤ 40%","Lifestyle, cardiac rehabilitation"]]],
{i:"Early reperfusion and evidence-based drug therapy have dramatically reduced MI mortality.",x:[["Complications and drugs",["VT/VF: amiodarone, lignocaine, DC shock","Heart block: atropine, pacing","LVF: furosemide, nitrates","Cardiogenic shock: noradrenaline, dobutamine, IABP, revascularisation"]]],f:"Chest pain + ST elevation\n → Aspirin + P2Y12 + anticoagulant + nitrate/morphine\n → Reperfusion: primary PCI (< 90 min) or thrombolysis (< 12 h)\n → β-blocker, statin, ACEI, MRA\n → Secondary prevention + rehabilitation",c:"MI treatment combines rapid reperfusion with antiplatelet, anticoagulant and neurohormonal therapy, followed by lifelong secondary prevention."});

Z("pharm7",7,"ph",["Antihypertensive drugs lower blood pressure to prevent stroke, heart attack, heart failure and kidney disease; first-line choices are ACEI/ARB, calcium channel blockers and thiazide diuretics, with β-blockers for specific indications.","BP = cardiac output × peripheral resistance; drugs lower BP by reducing volume (diuretics), heart output (β-blockers) or vessel tightness (ACEI, ARB, CCB, α-blockers, vasodilators).",["Classification: diuretics (thiazides — HCTZ, chlorthalidone, indapamide; loop; K⁺-sparing), RAS inhibitors (ACEI, ARB, aliskiren), CCBs (amlodipine), β-blockers (atenolol, metoprolol, nebivolol), α-blockers (prazosin), central sympatholytics (clonidine, methyldopa), vasodilators (hydralazine, minoxidil, nitroprusside).","Thiazides: inhibit Na⁺/Cl⁻ symporter in DCT; ↓ volume then ↓ PVR; ADR — hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, hyperuricaemia, hyperglycaemia, hyperlipidaemia, hypercalcaemia. β-blockers: ↓ CO, ↓ renin; ADR — bradycardia, bronchospasm, fatigue, masking hypoglycaemia.","Guidelines: start monotherapy/low-dose combination; target < 130/80 (most); combination ACEI/ARB + CCB ± thiazide; compelling indications.","Hypertensive emergency (BP > 180/120 + organ damage): IV labetalol, nicardipine, nitroprusside, esmolol, GTN — lower MAP ≤ 25% in 1st hour. Pregnancy: methyldopa, labetalol, nifedipine; avoid ACEI/ARB."],"Pregnancy HTN: 'Labetalol, Methyldopa, Nifedipine' ('Mothers Love Nifedipine')."],
"Hypertension is sustained elevation of BP ≥ 140/90 mmHg (≥ 130/80 by ACC/AHA); treatment reduces risk of stroke, MI, heart failure, CKD and death. BP is the product of cardiac output and total peripheral resistance, regulated by baroreceptor reflexes, sympathetic system, renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system and renal Na⁺ handling. Classification of antihypertensives: (1) diuretics — thiazides (hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone), thiazide-like (indapamide, metolazone), loop (furosemide, torsemide — in CKD, HF), K⁺-sparing (spironolactone, eplerenone, amiloride); (2) RAS inhibitors — ACE inhibitors (enalapril, ramipril, lisinopril), ARBs (losartan, telmisartan), direct renin inhibitor (aliskiren), ARNI; (3) calcium channel blockers — DHP (amlodipine, nifedipine, cilnidipine), non-DHP (verapamil, diltiazem); (4) β-adrenergic blockers — non-selective (propranolol), cardioselective (atenolol, metoprolol, bisoprolol), with vasodilator action (nebivolol, carvedilol, labetalol); (5) α-adrenergic blockers — prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin; phentolamine, phenoxybenzamine (pheochromocytoma); (6) central sympatholytics — clonidine, methyldopa, moxonidine; (7) vasodilators — arteriolar: hydralazine, minoxidil, diazoxide, fenoldopam; arteriolar + venous: sodium nitroprusside; (8) others — ganglion blockers and adrenergic neuron blockers (obsolete). Thiazide diuretics: inhibit Na⁺–Cl⁻ symporter in early distal convoluted tubule; initial fall in BP from reduced plasma volume and cardiac output; long-term effect from reduced peripheral resistance (Na⁺ depletion of vessel wall, reduced responsiveness to vasoconstrictors); low doses (HCTZ 12.5–25 mg, chlorthalidone 12.5–25 mg) are effective with few side effects; especially useful in elderly, isolated systolic hypertension, black patients, combination therapy; adverse effects — hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hyponatraemia, metabolic alkalosis, hyperuricaemia (gout), hyperglycaemia and insulin resistance, hyperlipidaemia, hypercalcaemia, erectile dysfunction, photosensitivity, sulfonamide allergy; less effective when GFR < 30 (except metolazone). β-blockers: lower BP by reducing cardiac output, inhibiting renin release, central action and resetting baroreceptors; no longer first-line for uncomplicated hypertension (less stroke protection, metabolic effects) but preferred with compelling indications — angina, post-MI, heart failure, tachyarrhythmias, migraine, essential tremor, thyrotoxicosis, pregnancy (labetalol); adverse effects — bradycardia, AV block, heart failure worsening (acute), bronchospasm (asthma — contraindicated for non-selective), cold extremities, fatigue, sleep disturbance, depression, sexual dysfunction, masking of hypoglycaemia symptoms in diabetics, dyslipidaemia, rebound hypertension and angina on abrupt withdrawal. Treatment guidelines (JNC 8, ESC/ESH 2023, ISH 2020, ACC/AHA 2017, Indian guidelines): lifestyle modification for all (DASH diet, salt < 5 g/day, weight reduction, exercise, limiting alcohol, stopping smoking); drug therapy for BP ≥ 140/90 (or ≥ 130/80 in high-risk); targets < 130/80 mmHg in most adults (< 140/90 minimum; systolic 130–139 in elderly); first-line classes — ACEI/ARB, CCB, thiazide-like diuretic (β-blocker with specific indications); start with a single-pill combination of two drugs (ACEI/ARB + CCB or + diuretic) for most patients; step-up to triple combination (ACEI/ARB + CCB + thiazide); resistant hypertension — add spironolactone, then β-blocker or α-blocker; compelling indications — diabetes/CKD with proteinuria (ACEI/ARB), heart failure (ACEI/ARB/ARNI, β-blocker, MRA, diuretic), post-MI (β-blocker, ACEI), BPH (α-blocker), black patients (CCB or thiazide), elderly (CCB, thiazide). Hypertensive crisis: (a) hypertensive emergency — severe hypertension (usually > 180/120) with acute target organ damage (hypertensive encephalopathy, intracranial haemorrhage, acute ischaemic stroke, acute LVF/pulmonary oedema, ACS, aortic dissection, eclampsia, AKI, malignant hypertension, pheochromocytoma crisis): ICU admission, IV drugs with titration — labetalol, nicardipine, clevidipine, sodium nitroprusside, nitroglycerin (ACS, pulmonary oedema), esmolol (aortic dissection — with nitroprusside; target SBP < 120 within 20 min), phentolamine (catecholamine crisis), hydralazine and MgSO4 (eclampsia), enalaprilat, fenoldopam; reduce MAP by no more than 25% in first hour, then to 160/100 over next 2–6 h, then normal over 24–48 h (avoid precipitous falls — ischaemia); (b) hypertensive urgency — severe BP without organ damage: oral agents (amlodipine, captopril, labetalol, clonidine) and gradual reduction over 24–48 h; sublingual nifedipine is not recommended. Hypertension in pregnancy (gestational hypertension, pre-eclampsia, chronic hypertension): treat BP ≥ 140/90 (severe ≥ 160/110 urgently); safe drugs — methyldopa (longest safety record; central α2 agonist), labetalol (first-line; oral/IV), nifedipine (extended-release oral), hydralazine (IV in severe), metoprolol; for severe pre-eclampsia/eclampsia — IV labetalol, IV hydralazine, oral nifedipine; magnesium sulphate for seizure prophylaxis; contraindicated — ACE inhibitors, ARBs, direct renin inhibitors (fetotoxic), nitroprusside (cyanide), diuretics (reduce plasma volume; avoided in pre-eclampsia), atenolol (fetal growth restriction); low-dose aspirin from 12 weeks for high-risk women to prevent pre-eclampsia; delivery is definitive treatment.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Diuretics","HCTZ, chlorthalidone, indapamide; furosemide; spironolactone"],["ACEI / ARB","Enalapril, ramipril; losartan, telmisartan"],["CCBs","Amlodipine, nifedipine; verapamil, diltiazem"],["β-blockers","Atenolol, metoprolol, nebivolol, carvedilol, labetalol"],["α-blockers","Prazosin, doxazosin"],["Central","Clonidine, methyldopa, moxonidine"],["Vasodilators","Hydralazine, minoxidil, nitroprusside"]]],
["Compelling indications",[["Condition","Preferred"],["Diabetes / CKD with proteinuria","ACEI/ARB"],["Heart failure","ACEI/ARB/ARNI, β-blocker, MRA, diuretic"],["Post-MI, angina","β-blocker, ACEI"],["Elderly, isolated systolic","CCB, thiazide"],["BPH","α-blocker"],["Pregnancy","Labetalol, methyldopa, nifedipine"],["Asthma","Avoid β-blockers"]]],
["Hypertensive emergency drugs",[["Drug","Notes"],["Labetalol IV","Most emergencies, pregnancy"],["Nicardipine IV","Stroke, general"],["Sodium nitroprusside","Potent; cyanide toxicity"],["Nitroglycerin IV","ACS, pulmonary oedema"],["Esmolol","Aortic dissection"],["Phentolamine","Pheochromocytoma"],["Hydralazine IV + MgSO4","Eclampsia"]]],
["Thiazide adverse effects",["Hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia","Hyponatraemia","Hyperuricaemia (gout)","Hyperglycaemia, hyperlipidaemia","Hypercalcaemia","Erectile dysfunction"]]],
{i:"Hypertension is the leading modifiable risk factor for cardiovascular death; most patients need two or more drugs to reach target.",x:[["Stepwise therapy",["Step 1: ACEI/ARB + CCB or thiazide (single pill)","Step 2: ACEI/ARB + CCB + thiazide","Step 3 (resistant): + spironolactone; then β-blocker/α-blocker","β-blockers whenever compelling indication"]],["Drugs to avoid in pregnancy",["ACE inhibitors, ARBs, aliskiren","Nitroprusside","Atenolol","Diuretics in pre-eclampsia"]]],f:"BP = CO × PVR\n ↓ Volume: diuretics\n ↓ CO: β-blockers\n ↓ PVR: ACEI/ARB, CCB, α-blockers, vasodilators, central sympatholytics\n Emergency: IV labetalol/nicardipine/nitroprusside → ↓ MAP ≤ 25% in 1 h",c:"Antihypertensive therapy is tailored to target BP, comorbidities and compelling indications, with combination therapy for most patients, controlled reduction in emergencies and pregnancy-safe agents for gestational hypertension."});

Z("pharm7",8,"ph",["Diuretics increase urine output by blocking Na⁺ reabsorption at different nephron sites; they are classified by potency and site of action.","Think of the nephron as a series of water-reclaiming stations; each diuretic class shuts a different station — the loop of Henle station is the biggest, so loop diuretics are the most powerful.",["High efficacy (loop): furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide — thick ascending limb, Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ symporter.","Medium efficacy: thiazides (HCTZ, chlorthiazide), thiazide-like (chlorthalidone, indapamide, metolazone) — DCT, Na⁺-Cl⁻ symporter.","Weak/adjunctive: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (acetazolamide — PCT); K⁺-sparing — aldosterone antagonists (spironolactone, eplerenone) and ENaC blockers (amiloride, triamterene) — collecting duct; osmotic (mannitol — PCT, loop).","Others: SGLT2 inhibitors (PCT; mild natriuresis), vasopressin antagonists (aquaretics — tolvaptan, conivaptan), xanthines."],"Efficacy: Loop > Thiazide > K⁺-sparing/CAI."],
"Diuretics are drugs that increase the excretion of Na⁺ and water (natriuresis); their efficacy depends on the fraction of filtered Na⁺ reabsorbed at the site they act and on compensatory reabsorption downstream. Classification: (1) high efficacy (high ceiling) — inhibitors of Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransport in thick ascending limb of loop of Henle: sulphamoyl derivatives (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide), ethacrynic acid (non-sulfonamide; ototoxic); can excrete up to 25% of filtered Na⁺; (2) medium efficacy — inhibitors of Na⁺–Cl⁻ symport in early distal tubule: thiazides (chlorothiazide, hydrochlorothiazide, benzthiazide, hydroflumethiazide), thiazide-like (chlorthalidone, indapamide, metolazone, xipamide) — 5–10% of filtered Na⁺; (3) weak or adjunctive diuretics — (a) carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (proximal tubule): acetazolamide, dorzolamide, brinzolamide; (b) potassium-sparing diuretics (late distal tubule and collecting duct): aldosterone antagonists (spironolactone, eplerenone, finerenone) and inhibitors of renal epithelial Na⁺ channel (amiloride, triamterene); (c) osmotic diuretics: mannitol, glycerol, isosorbide, urea; (d) methylxanthines (theophylline); (e) SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin) — proximal tubule; (4) aquaretics — vasopressin V2 receptor antagonists (tolvaptan, conivaptan) increase free water excretion (hyponatraemia, SIADH). Sites in nephron: proximal convoluted tubule (65–70% Na⁺ reabsorption) — CAIs, osmotic diuretics, SGLT2 inhibitors; descending limb — osmotic diuretics; thick ascending limb (20–25%) — loop diuretics; early distal tubule (5–8%) — thiazides; late distal tubule and collecting duct (2–5%) — K⁺-sparing; collecting duct (water) — ADH antagonists. Uses overview: oedema (CHF, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome), hypertension, acute pulmonary oedema, raised intracranial/intraocular pressure (mannitol, acetazolamide), hypercalcaemia (loop), nephrogenic DI and hypercalciuria (thiazides), hyperaldosteronism (spironolactone), glaucoma and altitude sickness (acetazolamide), forced diuresis in poisoning, heart failure (spironolactone reduces mortality). Electrolyte effects: loop and thiazides — hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis, hypomagnesaemia; loop — hypocalcaemia; thiazides — hypercalcaemia; K⁺-sparing — hyperkalaemia, metabolic acidosis; CAIs — metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia.",
[["Classification by efficacy and site",[["Class","Drugs","Site","Transporter"],["Loop (high)","Furosemide, torsemide, bumetanide","Thick ascending limb","Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻"],["Thiazide (medium)","HCTZ, chlorthalidone, indapamide, metolazone","Early DCT","Na⁺-Cl⁻"],["CAI (weak)","Acetazolamide","PCT","Carbonic anhydrase"],["Aldosterone antagonists","Spironolactone, eplerenone","Collecting duct","Mineralocorticoid receptor"],["ENaC blockers","Amiloride, triamterene","Collecting duct","ENaC"],["Osmotic","Mannitol","PCT, descending limb","Osmotic"],["Aquaretics","Tolvaptan","Collecting duct","V2 receptor"],["SGLT2 inhibitors","Dapagliflozin","PCT","SGLT2"]]],
["Electrolyte effects",[["Diuretic","K⁺","Ca²⁺ excretion","Acid–base"],["Loop","↓","↑","Alkalosis"],["Thiazide","↓","↓","Alkalosis"],["K⁺-sparing","↑","—","Acidosis"],["CAI","↓","—","Acidosis"]]]],
{f:"PCT (65%): CAI, mannitol, SGLT2i\n Thick ascending limb (25%): loop diuretics\n Early DCT (5–8%): thiazides\n Collecting duct (2–5%): spironolactone, amiloride; tolvaptan (water)",c:"Diuretics are classified by site and efficacy along the nephron; this determines their potency, electrolyte effects and clinical uses."});

Z("pharm7",9,"ph",["Furosemide (loop diuretic) and thiazides both promote salt and water loss: furosemide is powerful and fast, used in pulmonary oedema and severe oedema; thiazides are milder and long-acting, used mainly in hypertension.","Furosemide is a fire hose (high ceiling, works even in kidney failure); thiazide is a garden hose (steady, ideal for long-term BP control).",["Furosemide MOA: blocks Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ in thick ascending limb; also ↑ PGs → venodilatation (immediate relief in LVF); high ceiling; effective at low GFR.","Furosemide uses: acute pulmonary oedema, oedema (CHF, cirrhosis, nephrotic, renal), hypercalcaemia, hyperkalaemia, forced diuresis, hypertension with CKD/HF. ADR: hypokalaemia, hypovolaemia, hypotension, hypomagnesaemia, hypocalcaemia, hyperuricaemia, ototoxicity (with aminoglycosides), alkalosis, sulfa allergy.","Thiazide MOA: blocks Na⁺-Cl⁻ symporter in DCT; moderate efficacy; ineffective when GFR < 30 (except metolazone); ↓ urinary Ca²⁺.","Thiazide uses: hypertension (first-line), mild oedema, nephrogenic DI (paradoxical), idiopathic hypercalciuria/calcium stones, osteoporosis; ADR — hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, hyperuricaemia, hyperglycaemia, hyperlipidaemia, hypercalcaemia, ED."],"Loops Lose Calcium; Thiazides Take (retain) Calcium."],
"Furosemide (frusemide) is the prototype high-ceiling (loop) diuretic. Mechanism: secreted into the tubular lumen by organic anion transporter in PCT; binds the Cl⁻ site of the Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter (NKCC2) in luminal membrane of thick ascending limb, inhibiting reabsorption of Na⁺, K⁺ and Cl⁻; abolishes the lumen-positive potential, increasing Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ excretion; impairs medullary hypertonicity and both concentrating and diluting ability; increases renal blood flow by prostaglandin release; acute venodilatation (PG-mediated) reduces preload within minutes — relief of pulmonary oedema before diuresis begins. Pharmacokinetics: oral bioavailability ~60%, onset 30–60 min orally, 2–5 min IV, duration 4–6 h ('furosemide' = 'lasts six hours' — Lasix), highly protein bound, excreted unchanged; effect reduced by NSAIDs and probenecid. Uses: (1) oedema — CHF, acute pulmonary oedema (IV 40–80 mg), cirrhosis (with spironolactone), nephrotic syndrome, renal failure (effective even at low GFR; high doses needed); (2) hypertension — with renal insufficiency or HF, hypertensive emergency with fluid overload; (3) hypercalcaemia (after volume repletion with saline), hyperkalaemia; (4) acute renal failure (to convert oliguric to non-oliguric — controversial); (5) forced diuresis in poisoning (bromides, fluorides); (6) blood transfusion in CHF patients; (7) SIADH with hypertonic saline. Adverse effects: hypokalaemia (most common; arrhythmias, digoxin toxicity), hypomagnesaemia, hypocalcaemia, hyponatraemia, hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis, dehydration, hypovolaemia, hypotension, prerenal azotaemia, hyperuricaemia (gout), hyperglycaemia, dyslipidaemia, ototoxicity (tinnitus, deafness — rapid IV, high doses, with aminoglycosides), allergic reactions (sulfonamide), interstitial nephritis, pancreatitis. Interactions: aminoglycosides (ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity), digoxin (hypokalaemia), lithium (raised levels), NSAIDs (reduced effect), cephalosporins (nephrotoxicity), sulfonylureas, warfarin (displacement). Torsemide has better oral bioavailability and longer duration. Thiazides (hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone, indapamide): mechanism — inhibit Na⁺–Cl⁻ symporter (NCC) in early distal convoluted tubule; moderate natriuresis (5–8% of filtered load; flat dose–response curve); increase K⁺ and H⁺ secretion in collecting duct (more Na⁺ delivery, aldosterone); decrease Ca²⁺ excretion (enhanced basolateral Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchange); some carbonic anhydrase inhibition; reduce GFR (ineffective < 30 mL/min except metolazone); mild direct vasodilator; paradoxical antidiuresis in diabetes insipidus (volume contraction increases proximal reabsorption). Pharmacokinetics: oral; HCTZ duration 6–12 h; chlorthalidone 48–72 h (preferred for BP); indapamide 24 h. Uses: hypertension (first-line, low doses, in combination), mild to moderate oedema (CHF, cirrhosis, nephrotic; metolazone with loop for resistant oedema — sequential nephron blockade), nephrogenic and central diabetes insipidus, idiopathic hypercalciuria and recurrent calcium stones, osteoporosis (reduce bone loss), bromide intoxication. Adverse effects: hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hyponatraemia (especially elderly women), hypercalcaemia, hyperuricaemia and gout, hyperglycaemia (impaired insulin release — hypokalaemia), increased LDL and triglycerides, metabolic alkalosis, erectile dysfunction, photosensitivity rashes, pancreatitis, sulfonamide hypersensitivity, interstitial nephritis.",
[["Furosemide vs thiazide",[["Feature","Furosemide (loop)","Thiazide"],["Site","Thick ascending limb (NKCC2)","Early DCT (NCC)"],["Efficacy","High ceiling (25% Na⁺)","Moderate (5–8%)"],["Onset (IV/oral)","2–5 min / 30–60 min","1–2 h oral"],["Duration","4–6 h","6–72 h"],["Low GFR","Effective","Ineffective (< 30)"],["Urinary Ca²⁺","↑ (hypocalcaemia)","↓ (hypercalcaemia)"],["Main use","Pulmonary oedema, severe oedema","Hypertension"],["Special ADR","Ototoxicity","Hyperglycaemia, hyperlipidaemia"],["Common ADR","Hypokalaemia, alkalosis, hyperuricaemia","Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, hyperuricaemia"]]],
["Uses of furosemide",["Acute pulmonary oedema (IV)","Oedema: CHF, cirrhosis, nephrotic, CKD","Hypercalcaemia, hyperkalaemia","Hypertension with HF/CKD","Forced diuresis"]],
["Uses of thiazides",["Hypertension","Mild oedema","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Hypercalciuria, calcium stones","Osteoporosis"]]],
{c:"Loop and thiazide diuretics differ in site, efficacy and calcium handling: furosemide is used for rapid, powerful diuresis, whereas thiazides are preferred for long-term hypertension; both cause hypokalaemia that requires monitoring."});

Z("pharm7",10,"ph",["Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic that blocks aldosterone receptors in the collecting duct; it causes mild diuresis while retaining potassium, and reduces mortality in heart failure.","Aldosterone tells the kidney to hold sodium and dump potassium; spironolactone silences that message.",["MOA: competitive antagonist of mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) receptor in late DCT/collecting duct → ↓ ENaC and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase → ↑ Na⁺ excretion, ↓ K⁺ and H⁺ excretion.","Uses: HFrEF (↓ mortality — RALES), cirrhotic ascites (drug of choice), primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn's), resistant hypertension, nephrotic oedema, with loop/thiazide to prevent hypokalaemia, hirsutism, PCOS, acne.","ADR: hyperkalaemia (esp. with ACEI, CKD), gynaecomastia, impotence, menstrual irregularity (antiandrogenic), metabolic acidosis, GI upset; eplerenone is selective (no gynaecomastia).","CI: hyperkalaemia, renal failure, with K⁺ supplements; onset slow (days) — active metabolite canrenone."],"Spironolactone side effect: 'Spiro-gynaecomastia'."],
"Spironolactone is a synthetic steroid that acts as a competitive antagonist of aldosterone at the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) in principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct. Aldosterone normally induces synthesis of aldosterone-induced proteins that increase the number and activity of luminal epithelial Na⁺ channels (ENaC) and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, promoting Na⁺ reabsorption and K⁺ and H⁺ secretion; spironolactone prevents this, producing mild natriuresis with K⁺ and H⁺ retention. Its efficacy depends on circulating aldosterone levels (more effective in hyperaldosteronism). Also blocks androgen receptors and inhibits steroidogenesis (antiandrogenic). Pharmacokinetics: oral bioavailability 70%, extensively metabolised to active canrenone and 7α-thiomethylspironolactone (t½ 16–18 h), so onset is slow (2–3 days) and effects persist after stopping. Uses: (1) heart failure with reduced EF — low dose (25–50 mg) reduces mortality and hospitalisation (antifibrotic, blocks aldosterone escape); (2) oedema with secondary hyperaldosteronism — cirrhosis with ascites (drug of choice; 100–400 mg with furosemide in 100:40 ratio), nephrotic syndrome, CHF; (3) primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) — diagnosis, preoperative preparation, long-term in bilateral hyperplasia; (4) resistant hypertension (fourth drug); (5) with thiazide or loop diuretics to counteract hypokalaemia; (6) hirsutism, PCOS, acne, female pattern hair loss (antiandrogenic); (7) Liddle syndrome — not effective (use amiloride). Adverse effects: hyperkalaemia (dangerous especially with renal impairment, diabetes, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs, K⁺ supplements, β-blockers), metabolic acidosis, gynaecomastia, breast tenderness, decreased libido and impotence in men, menstrual irregularities and hirsutism reversal in women, drowsiness, GI upset (peptic ulcer), rashes, hyponatraemia. Eplerenone is more selective for MR (no antiandrogenic effects); finerenone is a non-steroidal MRA used in diabetic kidney disease. Other K⁺-sparing diuretics: amiloride and triamterene directly block ENaC independent of aldosterone (used in Liddle syndrome, lithium-induced nephrogenic DI). Contraindications: hyperkalaemia, severe renal failure (eGFR < 30), Addison disease, concomitant K⁺ supplements.",
[["Uses",["Heart failure (↓ mortality)","Cirrhosis with ascites (drug of choice)","Primary hyperaldosteronism","Resistant hypertension","Prevent hypokalaemia with loop/thiazide","Hirsutism, PCOS, acne"]],
["Spironolactone vs eplerenone vs amiloride",[["Feature","Spironolactone","Eplerenone","Amiloride"],["MOA","MR antagonist","Selective MR antagonist","ENaC blocker"],["Aldosterone dependent","Yes","Yes","No"],["Gynaecomastia","Yes","No","No"],["Onset","Slow (days)","Moderate","Rapid"],["Special use","Ascites, hirsutism","HF post-MI","Liddle syndrome, lithium DI"]]],
["Adverse effects",["Hyperkalaemia","Metabolic acidosis","Gynaecomastia, impotence","Menstrual irregularity","GI upset"]]],
{c:"Spironolactone is a weak diuretic but a powerful aldosterone antagonist, with mortality benefit in heart failure and key roles in ascites and hyperaldosteronism; hyperkalaemia and antiandrogenic effects are its main limitations."});

Z("pharm7",11,"ph",["Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic: a sugar alcohol that is filtered but not reabsorbed, holding water in the tubules and pulling water out of tissues such as the brain and eye.","Mannitol acts like a sponge in the urine and in the blood — it draws water from swollen brain cells into blood and then into urine.",["MOA: freely filtered, not reabsorbed → ↑ osmolality of tubular fluid → ↓ water (and Na⁺) reabsorption in PCT and descending limb; ↑ plasma osmolality → draws water from cells.","Given IV only (20% solution, 0.25–1 g/kg); not metabolised.","Uses: raised intracranial pressure (cerebral oedema, head injury), raised intraocular pressure (acute angle-closure glaucoma, before eye surgery), maintain urine flow (prevent ARF in haemolysis, rhabdomyolysis), forced diuresis in poisoning, dialysis disequilibrium.","ADR/CI: volume overload, pulmonary oedema (CI in CHF), hyponatraemia then hypernatraemia, dehydration, headache, nausea, rebound ICP; CI — anuria, active intracranial bleed, severe dehydration."],"Mannitol: 'osmotic sponge' for brain and eye."],
"Mannitol is a six-carbon polyhydric alcohol (sugar alcohol) used as an osmotic diuretic. Properties of an osmotic diuretic: freely filtered at glomerulus, not reabsorbed by tubules, pharmacologically inert, not metabolised. Mechanism: (1) in the kidney — mannitol in the tubular lumen raises the osmolality of tubular fluid, retaining water in proximal tubule and thin descending limb of loop of Henle (water-permeable segments); the resulting dilution of tubular Na⁺ reduces its reabsorption; washout of medullary hypertonicity (increased medullary blood flow) further reduces concentrating ability; it produces a large volume of dilute urine with increased excretion of water more than Na⁺ (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Cl⁻ and HCO3⁻ also increase); (2) systemically — because it does not cross cell membranes or the intact blood–brain barrier, it raises plasma osmolality and draws water from intracellular compartments (brain, eye) into the extracellular fluid, expanding ECF volume initially. Pharmacokinetics: not absorbed orally (causes osmotic diarrhoea), given IV as 10–20% solution; t½ 1–2 h (prolonged in renal failure); excreted unchanged in urine. Dose: 0.25–1 g/kg IV over 15–30 minutes; repeated 4–6 hourly guided by plasma osmolality (keep < 320 mOsm/kg). Uses: (1) reduction of raised intracranial pressure — cerebral oedema due to head injury, tumours, encephalitis, prior to neurosurgery (temporising measure before definitive treatment); (2) reduction of raised intraocular pressure — acute congestive (angle-closure) glaucoma, before ocular surgery; (3) maintenance of urine flow and prevention of acute renal failure in conditions with large solute load or reduced GFR — massive haemolysis (incompatible transfusion), rhabdomyolysis, cardiovascular surgery, severe trauma, shock (after volume correction) — evidence limited; (4) forced diuresis in poisoning (rarely now); (5) dialysis disequilibrium syndrome; (6) irrigation in transurethral prostatectomy (non-haemolytic). Adverse effects: expansion of ECF volume — circulatory overload, acute pulmonary oedema, heart failure (dangerous in CHF); initial dilutional hyponatraemia followed by hypernatraemia and dehydration with excessive water loss; hyperkalaemia; headache, nausea, vomiting, chills, fever; rebound rise in ICP (if mannitol enters brain through damaged BBB); acute kidney injury with high doses (osmotic nephrosis); extravasation causes tissue necrosis; hypersensitivity. Contraindications: anuria due to renal disease, acute tubular necrosis, severe dehydration, pulmonary congestion/CHF, active intracranial bleeding (except during craniotomy). Other osmotic agents: glycerol (oral; raised ICP/IOP; metabolised — hyperglycaemia), isosorbide (oral), urea (rarely), hypertonic saline (alternative for raised ICP).",
[["Key points",[["Feature","Detail"],["Nature","Sugar alcohol; filtered, not reabsorbed"],["Route","IV only (oral → diarrhoea)"],["Site","PCT, descending limb, (medulla washout)"],["Systemic effect","↑ Plasma osmolality → draws water from brain/eye"],["Dose","0.25–1 g/kg IV over 15–30 min"]]],
["Uses",["Raised intracranial pressure","Acute angle-closure glaucoma, pre-eye surgery","Prevent ARF in haemolysis, rhabdomyolysis","Dialysis disequilibrium","TURP irrigation"]],
["Contraindications",["CHF, pulmonary oedema","Anuria","Severe dehydration","Active intracranial bleed"]]],
{c:"Mannitol works through osmotic action in the tubules and plasma, making it valuable for raised intracranial and intraocular pressure; circulatory overload limits its use in heart failure."});

Z("pharm7",12,"ph",["Vasopressors and inotropes support circulation in shock: noradrenaline (first choice in septic shock) constricts vessels to raise BP, dopamine acts dose-dependently on dopamine, β1 and α receptors, and dobutamine increases heart contractility in cardiogenic shock.","Noradrenaline squeezes the pipes; dobutamine strengthens the pump; dopamine does a bit of each depending on dose.",["Shock types: hypovolaemic (fluids, blood), cardiogenic (dobutamine, noradrenaline, revascularisation), distributive/septic (fluids + noradrenaline ± vasopressin, hydrocortisone), anaphylactic (adrenaline IM), obstructive (relieve cause).","Noradrenaline: α1 > β1 → vasoconstriction, ↑ MAP; first-line vasopressor in septic shock (target MAP ≥ 65); ADR — tissue ischaemia, extravasation necrosis (phentolamine), arrhythmias.","Dopamine: low (2–5 µg/kg/min) D1 renal/mesenteric dilatation; medium (5–10) β1 inotropy; high (> 10) α1 vasoconstriction; more arrhythmias than NA.","Dobutamine: β1 agonist (racemic) → ↑ contractility, little ↑ HR/BP; cardiogenic shock, acute HF, stress echo; ADR — tachycardia, arrhythmias, hypotension. Also adrenaline, vasopressin, phenylephrine, milrinone."],"Septic shock: 'Fluids first, then NorAdrenaline'."],
"Shock is circulatory failure leading to inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery. Management principles: identify and treat the cause, restore intravascular volume (crystalloids 30 mL/kg in septic shock; blood in haemorrhage), oxygenation and ventilation, and vasoactive drugs to maintain mean arterial pressure (≥ 65 mmHg) and cardiac output, monitoring lactate, urine output and mental status. Vasoactive drugs: (1) vasopressors — noradrenaline, adrenaline, dopamine (high dose), phenylephrine, vasopressin, angiotensin II; (2) inotropes — dobutamine, dopamine (moderate dose), adrenaline, milrinone (PDE3 inhibitor), levosimendan. Noradrenaline: endogenous catecholamine; potent α1 agonist (arteriolar and venous constriction, raised systolic and diastolic BP, increased peripheral resistance) and β1 agonist (modest inotropy; HR may fall reflexly); negligible β2 action; IV infusion only (0.05–0.5 µg/kg/min) via central line (short t½ ~2 min); uses — first-line vasopressor in septic shock and vasodilatory shock, cardiogenic shock with severe hypotension, neurogenic shock, hypotension during anaesthesia; adverse effects — excessive vasoconstriction (peripheral, splanchnic and renal ischaemia, digital gangrene), hypertension, arrhythmias, reflex bradycardia, extravasation causing tissue necrosis (infiltrate with phentolamine), headache. Dopamine: endogenous precursor of NA acting on D1, β1 and α1 receptors in a dose-dependent manner — low dose (0.5–2/2–5 µg/kg/min): D1 receptors — renal, mesenteric and coronary vasodilatation, natriuresis ('renal dose' — no proven renal protection, no longer recommended); intermediate (5–10 µg/kg/min): β1 — increased contractility and HR, cardiac output, release of NA; high (> 10 µg/kg/min): α1 — vasoconstriction, increased BP; uses — cardiogenic shock, septic shock (second-line; more arrhythmias and mortality than NA in trials), symptomatic bradycardia unresponsive to atropine; adverse effects — tachycardia, arrhythmias, nausea, vomiting, angina, hypertension, tissue necrosis on extravasation. Dobutamine: synthetic catecholamine (racemic: (+) isomer β1 agonist and α1 antagonist; (−) isomer α1 agonist); net predominant β1 action with mild β2 — increases contractility and cardiac output with relatively little increase in heart rate, mild reduction in afterload; does not act on dopamine receptors; t½ 2 min; IV infusion 2–20 µg/kg/min; uses — cardiogenic shock (with noradrenaline if hypotensive), acute decompensated heart failure with low output, after cardiac surgery, septic shock with myocardial dysfunction, dobutamine stress echocardiography; adverse effects — tachycardia, arrhythmias, angina, hypotension (β2), tolerance with prolonged infusion (> 72 h). Adrenaline: α and β agonist; first-line in anaphylactic shock (0.5 mg IM, 1:1000) and cardiac arrest (1 mg IV every 3–5 min), second-line in septic shock. Vasopressin (V1 agonist): added to noradrenaline in refractory septic shock (low dose 0.03 U/min). Phenylephrine: pure α1 agonist; hypotension during anaesthesia, spinal anaesthesia. Milrinone: PDE3 inhibitor — inodilator; acute HF. Corticosteroids: IV hydrocortisone 200 mg/day in septic shock requiring escalating vasopressors.",
[["Receptor actions",[["Drug","α1","β1","β2","D1","Main effect"],["Noradrenaline","+++","++","0","0","Vasoconstriction, ↑ BP"],["Adrenaline","+++","+++","++","0","Inotropy + vasoconstriction"],["Dopamine (low)","0","0","0","+++","Renal dilatation"],["Dopamine (medium)","+","++","0","++","Inotropy"],["Dopamine (high)","+++","++","0","++","Vasoconstriction"],["Dobutamine","+","+++","+","0","Inotropy"],["Phenylephrine","+++","0","0","0","Vasoconstriction"]]],
["Choice by shock type",[["Shock","Drug"],["Septic","Fluids + noradrenaline (± vasopressin, hydrocortisone)"],["Cardiogenic","Dobutamine ± noradrenaline; revascularisation"],["Anaphylactic","Adrenaline IM 0.5 mg"],["Hypovolaemic","Fluids, blood (vasopressors temporary)"],["Neurogenic","Fluids, noradrenaline/phenylephrine, atropine"]]]],
{c:"Vasopressor and inotrope choice is matched to shock physiology: noradrenaline for vasodilatory shock, dobutamine for pump failure and adrenaline for anaphylaxis, after adequate fluid resuscitation."});

Z("pharm7",13,"ph",["Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is raised pressure in lung arteries; drugs dilate pulmonary vessels via three pathways — endothelin receptor antagonists, nitric oxide–cGMP enhancers and prostacyclin analogues — plus supportive therapy.","PAH is a three-lane problem: too much endothelin (constrictor), too little NO and too little prostacyclin (dilators) — each drug class fixes one lane.",["Endothelin receptor antagonists: bosentan, ambrisentan, macitentan (hepatotoxic, teratogenic).","NO–cGMP pathway: PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil), soluble guanylyl cyclase stimulator (riociguat); inhaled NO (newborns, acute).","Prostacyclin pathway: epoprostenol (IV), treprostinil, iloprost (inhaled), selexipag (oral IP receptor agonist).","Others: CCBs (nifedipine, diltiazem — only vasoreactive patients), diuretics, oxygen, anticoagulants, digoxin; sotatercept (activin signalling inhibitor); lung transplant."],"PAH drugs: 'ERA + PDE5i + Prostacyclin'."],
"Pulmonary arterial hypertension (WHO group 1) is defined by mean pulmonary arterial pressure > 20 mmHg at rest with pulmonary vascular resistance > 2 Wood units and normal wedge pressure; it results from endothelial dysfunction with vasoconstriction, smooth muscle proliferation and in situ thrombosis, eventually causing right heart failure. Pathobiology involves three pathways: excess endothelin-1 (vasoconstrictor, mitogen), deficient nitric oxide (and cGMP), and deficient prostacyclin (PGI2). Drugs: (1) endothelin receptor antagonists (ERAs) — bosentan (ETA/ETB dual; hepatotoxic — monthly LFTs; interacts with CYP inducers), ambrisentan (ETA selective; peripheral oedema), macitentan (dual; less hepatotoxic); all teratogenic (contraception required), cause anaemia and oedema; (2) phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors — sildenafil (20 mg TDS), tadalafil (40 mg OD); inhibit cGMP breakdown in pulmonary vasculature; ADR — headache, flushing, dyspepsia, visual disturbance, epistaxis; contraindicated with nitrates and riociguat; (3) soluble guanylyl cyclase stimulator — riociguat (also for chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension; hypotension; teratogenic); (4) prostacyclin pathway — epoprostenol (continuous IV infusion via central line; very short t½; most effective in severe disease; jaw pain, flushing, diarrhoea, line sepsis; rebound on interruption), treprostinil (SC, IV, inhaled, oral), iloprost (inhaled 6–9 times daily), beraprost (oral), selexipag (oral selective IP receptor agonist); (5) calcium channel blockers — high-dose nifedipine, diltiazem or amlodipine only for patients with positive acute vasoreactivity test (~10% of idiopathic PAH); verapamil avoided (negative inotropy); (6) activin signalling inhibitor — sotatercept (newer); (7) supportive therapy — oxygen to maintain saturation > 90%, diuretics for right heart failure, oral anticoagulants (selected idiopathic cases), digoxin for AF/RV failure, exercise rehabilitation, vaccination, contraception; (8) interventional — balloon atrial septostomy, lung or heart–lung transplantation. Treatment strategy: initial combination therapy (ERA + PDE5 inhibitor, e.g., ambrisentan + tadalafil) for most patients; add prostacyclin pathway drug if inadequate response or high risk (IV epoprostenol in high-risk). Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn is treated with inhaled nitric oxide, sildenafil and supportive care. Pulmonary hypertension secondary to left heart or lung disease is treated by managing the underlying cause.",
[["Drug classes",[["Pathway","Drugs","Key ADR"],["Endothelin","Bosentan, ambrisentan, macitentan","Hepatotoxicity, teratogenicity, oedema, anaemia"],["NO–cGMP","Sildenafil, tadalafil; riociguat","Headache, hypotension; CI with nitrates"],["Prostacyclin","Epoprostenol, treprostinil, iloprost, selexipag","Flushing, jaw pain, diarrhoea; line sepsis"],["CCBs","Nifedipine, diltiazem, amlodipine","Only vasoreactive patients"],["Supportive","O2, diuretics, anticoagulants, digoxin","—"]]]],
{c:"PAH therapy targets the endothelin, nitric oxide and prostacyclin pathways, increasingly in combination, with supportive measures and transplantation for advanced disease."});
